<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Fortriu</h1><p class="st-meta">300 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Pictish kingdom, whose kings dominated northern Britain in the 7th to 9th centuries and whose heartland is now placed around Moray.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fortriu takes its name from the Verturiones, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> in the 360s as one of the two main peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. In early Irish annals 'king of Fortriu' became the title of the most powerful Pictish rulers. Long located in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>, it is now usually placed around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-moray">Moray</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a>, following work by Alex Woolf.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings of Fortriu built an over-kingship across <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Pictland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-beli">Bridei son of Beli</a> destroyed the Northumbrian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Dun Nechtain</a> in 685, ending English dominance in the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts">Óengus son of Fergus</a>, from 729 to 761, defeated rivals and even campaigned against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and Strathclyde. Major centres included the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghead">Burghead</a> and the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a>. Later kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caustantin-mac-fergusa">Constantín son of Fergus</a> also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 839 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> army killed the king of Fortriu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganan-mac-oengusa">Eóganán</a>, with many nobles. In the aftermath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Cináed mac Ailpín</a> seized power, and Fortriu merged into the Gaelic kingdom of Alba around 900. The region later re-emerged as the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-moray">Moray</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-fortriu.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Fortriu" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Users Angusmclellan, Angusmclellan, Angusmclellan on en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Medieval_Scotland_areas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bridei son of Beli, Óengus son of Fergus, Constantín son of Fergus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pictish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fortriu, Verturiones</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=kingdom-of-fortriu" aria-label="Kingdom of Fortriu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4590 231 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Fortriu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4910 487L4892 500M4910 487L4909 487"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4892 500h0M4909 487h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 487h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 487h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=600&amp;focus=kingdom-of-fortriu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
